 # Style Transfer and Illustrative Rendering
 
-Also called: Style Transfer Functions (STF), Illustrative Volume Rendering with Style Transfer
+Also called: Style Transfer Functions (STF)
 
 ## Overview
-This family extends standard direct volume ray casting by replacing or augmenting photorealistic color mapping with artistic style descriptors, enabling non-photorealistic rendering (NPR) of volumetric data [S1, 11445-illustrative-volume-rendering-with-style-transfer]. It differs from sibling methods by mapping scalar values to style indices or descriptors rather than direct RGB colors, allowing the application of pre-computed artistic textures (such as lit spheres or matcaps) and illustrative effects like contour lines and transparency modulation [11409-raycasting-with-style-transfer-and-contour-lines, 11445-illustrative-volume-rendering-with-style-transfer].
+This family extends standard direct volume ray casting by replacing or augmenting photorealistic color mapping with artistic style descriptors, enabling non-photorealistic rendering (NPR) of volumetric data [S1]. It differs from sibling methods by mapping scalar values to style indices or descriptors rather than direct RGB colors, allowing the application of pre-computed artistic textures (such as lit spheres or matcaps) and illustrative effects like contour lines and transparency modulation [S1].
 
 ## Shared principles
-All methods in this family share the following core mechanism, building on [Transfer Function and Opacity Mapping](2789-transfer-function-and-opacity-mapping.md):
-
-1.  **Input Domain**: The method operates on scalar volume values $V(\mathbf{p})$ retrieved at sample positions $\mathbf{p}$ along the ray [S2, 2789].
-2.  **Mapping Function**: A transfer function $T$ maps the scalar value $V$ to a color vector $\mathbf{c} \in [0,1]^3$ and an opacity scalar $\alpha \in [0,1]$ [S2, 2789].
-    $$ T: V \rightarrow (\mathbf{c}, \alpha) $$
-3.  **Composition**: The resulting $\mathbf{c}$ and $\alpha$ are used in the front-to-back compositing equation defined in the parent article [19-direct-volume-ray-casting.md] to compute the final pixel color [S2, 2789].
-4.  **Parameterization**: Transfer functions are typically parameterized by control points $(V_k, \mathbf{c}_k, \alpha_k)$, with interpolation (linear, cubic, or piecewise) between points [S2, 2789].
 
